Let $z = x - y$. The hypothesis $F(x) = F(y)$ for all bounded linear functionals $F$ on $X$ means, by linearity of $F$,

$$F(z) = F(x) - F(y) = 0 \quad \text{for every } F \in X^*.$$

We claim $z = 0$. Suppose for contradiction that $z \neq 0$. By the Hahn–Banach theorem (in the form guaranteed by the analytic version, which applies to any normed linear space, not just Banach spaces), there exists a bounded linear functional $F_0 \in X^*$ with

$$F_0(z) = \|z\| \neq 0 \quad \text{and} \quad \|F_0\| = 1.$$

Concretely, define $f$ on $\operatorname{span}\{z\}$ by $f(\alpha z) = \alpha\|z\|$; this is a bounded linear functional of norm $1$ on the one-dimensional subspace $\operatorname{span}\{z\}$, and Hahn–Banach extends it to all of $X$ without increasing its norm, yielding $F_0$.

But then $F_0(z) = \|z\| \neq 0$, contradicting $F(z) = 0$ for all $F \in X^*$. Hence $z = 0$, i.e., $x = y$. $\blacksquare$

